---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const locale = "en";
---

<Base
  title="Install the app · This Indian Life"
  description="Install This Indian Life as an app on your phone or computer — for a cleaner, full-screen read that also works offline. Step-by-step instructions for Android, iPhone, and desktop."
>
  <div class="wrap about">
    <Nav locale={locale} />

    <header class="domain-head">
      <span class="lbl">Install the app</span>
      <h1 class="serif">Keep This Indian Life one tap away.</h1>
      <p class="muted">
        This site can be installed as an app — a clean, full-screen version with no browser
        clutter, that also keeps the pages you've visited readable offline. It's the same
        site, just nicer to live with. Nothing to download from a store, and no notifications.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="sec-hd">The quick way</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>If your browser supports one-click install, the button below will appear. If it
        doesn't show up, your browser just does it a little differently — the per-device
        steps further down cover every case.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="install-cta">
        <button class="btn dom-people" id="pwa-install-btn" type="button" hidden>Install the app</button>
        <p class="muted install-note" id="pwa-install-note"></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="sec-hd">On Android (Chrome)</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <ol>
          <li>Tap the <strong>⋮</strong> menu in the top-right of Chrome.</li>
          <li>Choose <strong>Add to Home screen</strong> (sometimes shown as <em>Install app</em>).</li>
          <li>Confirm, and the icon lands on your home screen like any other app.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="sec-hd">On iPhone & iPad (Safari)</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <ol>
          <li>Tap the <strong>Share</strong> button (the square with an upward arrow) at the bottom of Safari.</li>
          <li>Scroll down and tap <strong>Add to Home Screen</strong>.</li>
          <li>Tap <strong>Add</strong> in the top-right. Done.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted">iPhones only allow this from Safari, not Chrome or other browsers.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="sec-hd">On a computer (Chrome, Edge, Brave)</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <ol>
          <li>Look for the small <strong>install icon</strong> on the right edge of the address bar (a monitor with a downward arrow).</li>
          <li>Click it, then click <strong>Install</strong>.</li>
          <li>The site opens in its own window and shows up alongside your other apps.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted">No install icon? Open the browser menu and look for <em>Install This Indian Life…</em>. Firefox and Safari on desktop don't support installing web apps.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="sec-hd">Why bother</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Honestly? You don't have to. The website works perfectly in a normal tab and always
        will. Installing just gives you a faster, full-screen, distraction-free version that
        survives a patchy connection — handy if you read on the move. That's the whole pitch.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Footer locale={locale} />
  </div>
</Base>
